# /etc/pam.d/away
# This file is the standard PAM (Pluggable Authentication Modules) configuration file.
# It includes the common configuration stacks for authentication, account management, and session handling.
# These included files are typically located in /etc/pam.d/ and defined by the system's PAM packages
# (e.g., libpam-common, pam-modules on Debian/Ubuntu).
#
# Since the 'away' package schema contains no fields, no variables can be injected.
# This template is a static, self-documenting wrapper around the standard Unix auth modules.

# Authentication management
# Includes common authentication settings (password policies, account validation, etc.)
@include common-auth

# Account management
# Includes common account settings (account validity, access controls, etc.)
@include common-account

# Session management
# Includes common session settings (resource limits, logging, etc.)
@include common-session